alue":"We want to determine the minimum and maximum altitude, and the satellite’s speed at those two locations. The altitude of the satellite is its height above the Earth’s surface. The radius of the Earth is 6378.12 km.","position":{"start":{"line":55,"column":1},"end":{"line":55,"column":1}},"key":"V8HPbVbhCu"}],"key":"XQSXjDtHR9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To avoid confusion with the ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"fBbFOG1QYt"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"y","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">y</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.625em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.03588em;\">y</span></span></span></span>","key":"T7zR6d7U6y"},{"type":"text","value":" coordinate, we will use capital ","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"hfuIp8I8l3"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Y","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"r3Gnt5G6oX"},{"type":"text","value":" for the state vector. Remember that the state vector includes 6 components, 3 positions and 3 velocities. These all need to be stored in one array.","position":{"start":{"line":57,"column":1},"end":{"line":57,"column":1}},"key":"exfzIoZfYX"}],"key":"rsfUL2Yjl6"},{"type":"tabSet","children":[{"type":"tabItem","title":"Python","sync":"python","children":[{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"G = 6.67430e-20  # km**3/(kg * s**2)\nm_1 = 5.97219e24  # kg\nm_2 = 1000  # kg\nmu = G * m_1\nR_E = 6378.12  # km\n\nr_0 = np.array((8000, 0, 6000))  # km\nv_0 = np.array((0, 7, 0))  # km/s\nY_0 = np.hstack((r_0, v_0))","key":"fHpLgDaxg2"},{"type":"outputs","id":"ydx3Ydx4gVgg6tepAnU8V","children":[],"key":"tou5hMK4B0"}],"key":"q3VtksTrdc"}],"key":"RnJLMvczFH"},{"type":"tabItem","title":"Matlab","sync":"matlab","children":[{"type":"include","file":"assets/two_body_relative_numerical_solution.m","literal":true,"filter":{"startAfter":"[section-2]","endBefore":"[section-3]"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"function two_body_relative_numerical_solution\n    G = 6.67430e-20; % km^3/(kg * s^2)\n    m1 = 5.97219e24; % kg\n    m2 = 1000; % kg\n    mu = G * m1; % km^3/s^2\n    RE = 6378.12; % km\n\n    r0 = [8000 0 6000]; % km\n    v0 = [0 7 0]; % km/s\n    Y0 = [r0 v0];\n","lang":"matlab","filename":"two_body_relative_numerical_solution.m","key":"XJAOEJrGrs"}],"key":"ubfRmYSb34"}],"key":"VIZanPVYu4"}],"key":"Mnj6zl6yqA"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Then, we need to define the function that describes the motion of our system. This function needs to compute the derivative of the state vector and return it to the solver.","position":{"start":{"line":86,"column":1},"end":{"line":86,"column":1}},"key":"RaNLKQFSwf"}],"key":"wE3TXW9mE8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Inside the function, we use the values in the state vector to fill the ","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"E6jEE8IbyS"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ydot","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"rCPeMk4oYT"},{"type":"text","value":" vector. Then, we return ","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"SM4L36Bo1t"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Ydot","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"eSRynwzMX7"},{"type":"text","value":" back to the solver.","position":{"start":{"line":88,"column":1},"end":{"line":88,"column":1}},"key":"QUlVJNlPR2"}],"key":"Pq2AMssqRN"},{"type":"tabSet","children":[{"type":"tabItem","title":"Python","sync":"python","children":[{"type":"block","kind":"notebook-code","children":[{"type":"code","lang":"python","executable":true,"value":"def relative_motion(t, Y):\n    \"\"\"Calculate the motion of a two-body system relative to $m_1$.\n\n    The state vector ``Y`` should be in the order:\n\n    1. Coordinates of $m_2$ relative to $m_1$\n    2. Velocity components of $m_2$ relative to $m_1$\n    \"\"\"\n    # Get the three position components\n    r_vec = Y[:3]\n\n    # Create the derivative vector and copy the velocities into it\n    Ydot = np.zeros_like(Y)\n    Ydot[:3] = Y[3:]\n\n    # Calculate the accelerations\n    r = np.sqrt(np.sum(np.square(r_vec)))\n    a_vec = -mu * r_vec / r**3\n    Ydot[3:] = a_vec\n\n    return Ydot","key":"FAJCTEMwyH"},{"type":"outputs","id":"5ZkXNPs1CtPojAo1o0XSZ","children":[],"key":"QlRiGqW42w"}],"key":"sXe7CjpbNX"}],"key":"jADZxzzg9v"},{"type":"tabItem","title":"Matlab","sync":"matlab","children":[{"type":"include","file":"assets/two_body_relative_numerical_solution.m","literal":true,"filter":{"startAfter":"[section-3]","endBefore":"[section-4]"},"children":[{"type":"code","value":"    function Ydot = relative_motion(t,Y)\n        rvector = Y(1:3);\n        vvector = Y(4:6);\n        r = norm(rvector);\n        avector = -mu .* rvector ./ r.^3;\n        Ydot = [vvector; avector];\n    end","lang":"matlab","filename":"two_body_relative_numerical_solution.m","key":"euxh1EVyiF"}],"key":"pAxStJqJOK"}],"key":"XO6gRNCwGT"}],"key":"iXTe4QiLvR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":129,"column":1},"end":{"line":129,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"With the function defined, we can call the sol